# SABOTAGE: T123 gate sabotage — the abbrev allocator's cross-host collision guard
# (build-integrity). `Samen.Abbrev.Allocator.propose/3`'s `free?` closure is reverted to
# its pre-T123 HOST-BLIND shape: it inspects only the CALLING host's own namespace
# (`host_ns`) + the legacy flat `global` map, never any OTHER host's namespace. That is
# exactly the bug that let propose auto-hand-back an abbrev already owned by a different
# host for a different module (T44-T47; T47 persisted it and tripped
# flatten_conflicts/1's fail-closed "LOSSY FLATTENING" raise, breaking every
# `use Samen.Resource` compile). With the union check neutralized, propose again returns
# the cross-host-owned "xyz", so the T123 red-paths MUST fail.
# APP: samen_core
# TEST_FILES: test/abbrev_allocator_cross_host_test.exs
# MUST_FAIL: propose for host A skips host B's "xyz" and picks a fresh abbrev
# MUST_FAIL: the pre-T123 host-blind check WOULD have returned the colliding "xyz"
diff --git a/samen_core/lib/samen/abbrev/allocator.ex b/samen_core/lib/samen/abbrev/allocator.ex
--- a/samen_core/lib/samen/abbrev/allocator.ex
+++ b/samen_core/lib/samen/abbrev/allocator.ex
@@ -78,7 +78,7 @@ defmodule Samen.Abbrev.Allocator do
     # auto-hand-back an abbrev already owned by a different host for an unrelated module
     # (T44-T47; T47 persisted it and tripped flatten_conflicts/1's fail-closed raise,
     # breaking every `use Samen.Resource` compile — see moduledoc, ADR-023, ADR-025).
-    free? = fn abbrev -> Enum.all?(owners_of(ns, abbrev), &(&1 == owner)) end
+    free? = fn abbrev -> Enum.all?(Enum.reject([Map.get(host_ns, abbrev), Map.get(global, abbrev)], &is_nil/1), &(&1 == owner)) end

     candidates = candidate_stream(owner)

